.SH "DDC *.opt FILE SYNTAX"
.IX Header "DDC *.opt FILE SYNTAX"
This manpage describes the syntax of *.opt index
option files (\*(L"opt-files\*(R") used by the \s-1DDC\s0 corpus indexing system.
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
.Vb 2
\& ##\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
\& ## Option Processor Directives
\& 
\& #comment
\& include "OPTFILE"
\&
\& ##\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
\& ## Required Declarations
\& 
\& LANG
\& IndexType TYPE
\&
\& ##\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
\& ## Boolean Flags and Switches
\& 
\& Utf8
\& MemoryMap
\& CaseInsensitive
\& DisableDefaultQueryLexicalExpansion
\& ShowNumberOfRelevantDocuments
\& QueryOnlyFiles
\& NoContextOperator
\& AllowUnsafeQueries
\& AllowCountByTokenAttribute
\& OutputBibliographyOfHits
\& LemmaQueryUsesMorphPattern
\& 
\& IndexChunks
\& IndexMorphPatterns
\& IndexPunctuation
\& UseDwdsThesaurus
\& UseParagraphTagToDivide
\& EmptyLineIsNotSentenceDelim
\& DontUseIndention
\& ArchiveIndex
\& ResumeOnIndexErrors
\& 
\& GutenbergInterface
\& DwdsCorpusInterface
\&
\& ##\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
\& ## Single\-valued Options
\& 
\& Indices INDEXLIST
\& IndexAlias FROM TO
\& IndicesToShow SHOWLIST
\& DefaultBibl NAME
\& HitBorders BREAKLIST
\& 
\& HtmlHighlighting L;R;LL;RR
\& TextHighlighting L;R;LL;RR
\& TableHighlighting L;R;LL;RR
\& TokenDelimiter STRING
\& InterpDelimiter STRING
\& 
\& RightKwicContextSize N
\& LeftKwicContextSize N
\& NumberOfKwicLinesInSnippets N
\& MaxRegExpExpansionSize N
\& MaxCachedHitsCount N
\& MaxQueryCacheSize N
\& UserMaxTokenCountInOnePeriod N
\& 
\& LocalPathPrefix VAL
\& InternetPathPrefix VAL
\& 
\& TfIdfRank FLOAT
\& PositionRank FLOAT
\& NearRank FLOAT
\& 
\& ServerInfo KEY VALUE
\& ServerInfoFile KEY FILENAME
\&
\& ##\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
\& ## Multi\-valued Options
\& 
\& textarea ...
\& Bibl ...
\& Expand ...
\& ExpandBibl ...
\& DefaultQueryIndex ...
\& Bigrams ...
.Ve
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
This section contains descriptions of the various options and flags
which may occur in a \s-1DDC\s0 corpus opt-file, insofar as the author is able
to provide them.  These are for the most part legacy options which
may or may not be fully functional.  I have not tested (most of)
these options for functionality.  The reader is strongly advised
to perform his or her own tests before using any of the options
described below (but particularly the undocumented ones)
in a production environment.
.PP
At runtime, \s-1DDC\s0 loads exactly one \*(L"top-level\*(R" corpus opt-file (\f(CW\*(C`CORPUS.opt\*(C'\fR)
for each physical (sub)corpus inventory \f(CW\*(C`CORPUS.con\*(C'\fR.  Typically, the opt-files
for physical subcorpora (\*(L"shards\*(R") will contain nothing more than an
\&\*(L"include\*(R" directive pointing to a superordinate shared options file.
.PP
Note that server option-files (\fIddc_server.opt\fR) as read
by the \f(CW\*(C`ddc_daemon\*(C'\fR program are documented independently;
see \fIddc_server.opt\fR\|(5) for details.
.SS "Sources"
.IX Subsection "Sources"
\&\s-1DDC\s0 opt-files are parsed and processed by the method
\&\fICConcIndexator::LoadOptionsFromString()\fR
after intialization of
default option values by the method
\&\fICConcIndexator::InitDefaultOptions()\fR,
both of which methods are defined in
\&\fIsrc/ConcordLib/ConcordOptions.cpp\fR.
Refer to the source code for further enlightenment.
.SS "Option Processor Directives"
.IX Subsection "Option Processor Directives"
\fI#comment\fR
.IX Subsection "#comment"
.PP
Comments are lines starting with a literal hash-mark (\f(CW\*(C`#\*(C'\fR).
Such lines are ignored by the option processor
\&\fBexcept\fR for the backwards-compatible #include directive.
.PP
\fIinclude\fR
.IX Subsection "include"
.PP
.Vb 2
\& include "OPTFILE"
\& #include "OPTFILE"
.Ve
.PP
The \f(CW\*(C`include\*(C'\fR directive inserts the contents of an external opt-file at the
current position.  If \s-1OPTFILE\s0 is a relative pathname, it is interpreted relative
to the directory containing the top-level opt-file for the current physical (sub)corpus.
.PP
Note that for historical reasons, the C preprocessor syntax \f(CW\*(C`#include "OPTFILE"\*(C'\fR
is also supported, is \fB\s-1NOT\s0\fR a comment, and \fB\s-1WILL\s0\fR be evaluated.  This behavior
may change in the future.
.SS "Required Declarations"
.IX Subsection "Required Declarations"
Each \s-1DDC\s0 option file requires two obligatory declarations \s-1LANG\s0 and IndexType, which
should occur as the first two options in an opt-file (although they
may be defined by an external file loaded with the include directive).
.PP
\fIIndexType\fR
.IX Subsection "IndexType"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& IndexType TYPE
.Ve
.PP
Sets the type of the underlying corpus index.
\&\fI\s-1TYPE\s0\fR can be \f(CW\*(C`DWDS_Index\*(C'\fR,  \f(CW\*(C`MorphXML_Index\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`Free_Index\*(C'\fR, or \f(CW\*(C`TabFormat_Index\*(C'\fR.
.PP
Future versions of \s-1DDC\s0 may support only the \f(CW\*(C`TabFormat_Index\*(C'\fR type; see
\&\fIddc_tabs\fR\|(5) for details on the \f(CW\*(C`TabFormat_Index\*(C'\fR format.
.PP
\fI\s-1LANG\s0\fR
.IX Subsection "LANG"
.PP
Sets language to use for runtime lexical expansion and possibly index-time analysis.
Known languages are \f(CW\*(C`German\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`Russian\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`English\*(C'\fR, and (possibly) \f(CW\*(C`Generic\*(C'\fR.
.SS "Boolean Flags and Switches"
.IX Subsection "Boolean Flags and Switches"
The following options represent boolean flags and switches, which
are set if and only if the corresponding flag appears in the corpus opt-file.
As of ddc v2.0.20, each boolean flag may appear with an optional value argument.
The value arguments \f(CW\*(C`no\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`n\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`false\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`off\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`disabled\*(C'\fR, and \f(CW0\fR cause the corresponding
boolean flag to be cleared.  Omitting the value argument or specifying any other value causes
the flag to be set.
.PP
Prior to ddc v2.0.20,
there was no way to override a previously set boolean flag:
to disable a boolean option backwards-compatibly, omit it from the opt-file or comment it out.
.PP
\fIUtf8\fR
.IX Subsection "Utf8"
.PP
If specified true, \s-1DDC\s0 expects all corpus data and external queries to be encoded in \s-1UTF\-8.\s0
This uses both iconv and C99 locales internally, so you should ensure that your
\&\s-1LC_CTYPE\s0 variable has a \s-1UTF\-8\s0 encoding (e.g. \fIexport LC_CTYPE=\*(L"en_US.UTF\-8\*(R"\fR in the parent shell.)
if you choose to use this option.
.PP
\fIMemoryMap\fR
.IX Subsection "MemoryMap"
.PP
If specified ande true, \s-1DDC\s0 will attempt to map runtime index data from the filesystem into virtual memory on startup.
If unspecified or false, \s-1DDC\s0 will fall back to the pre\-v2.1.12 behavior of loading runtime index data into
resident memory on startup, which tends to be both slow and resource-hungry.
Use of the MemoryMap option requires a working \fImmap()\fR system call and compile-time support in the underlying \s-1DDC\s0 library.
.PP
Since ddc v2.1.12.
.PP
\fIDisableDefaultQueryLexicalExpansion\fR
.IX Subsection "DisableDefaultQueryLexicalExpansion"
.PP
If specified and true, \s-1DDC\s0 will not include the \f(CW\*(C`infl\*(C'\fR term expander to the default expansion
pipeline for the \f(CW\*(C`Token\*(C'\fR index.
.PP
Note that a term expansion explicitly defined with an Expand clause for
the \f(CW\*(C`Token\*(C'\fR index will override the effects of this option.
.PP
\fICaseInsensitive\fR
.IX Subsection "CaseInsensitive"
.PP
If specified and true, \s-1DDC\s0 will not add the \f(CW\*(C`case\*(C'\fR term expander to the default expansion
pipeline for the \f(CW\*(C`Token\*(C'\fR index.
.PP
Note that a term expansion explicitly defined with an Expand clause for
the \f(CW\*(C`Token\*(C'\fR index will override the effects of this option.
.PP
\fIShowNumberOfRelevantDocuments\fR
.IX Subsection "ShowNumberOfRelevantDocuments"
.PP
(from DDCReadme):
If set, \s-1DDC\s0 calculates the number of relevant documents, otherwise the member that holds this number is set to 0.
.PP
\fIQueryOnlyFiles\fR
.IX Subsection "QueryOnlyFiles"
.PP
(from DDCReadme):
If set, \s-1DDC\s0 doesn't create a sentence break collection.
Only meaningful for \f(CW\*(C`DWDS_Index\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`MorphXML_Index\*(C'\fR Index Types.
.PP
\fINoContextOperator\fR
.IX Subsection "NoContextOperator"
.PP
(from DDCReadme):
If set, prohibits context operator (#CNTXT) in the query language.
.PP
\fIAllowUnsafeQueries\fR
.IX Subsection "AllowUnsafeQueries"
.PP
Unless this option is set to a true value,
any attempt to compile a potentially unsafe query will cause a runtime
exception to be thrown.  Currently, only file-list queries (\f(CW\*(C`< FILENAME\*(C'\fR)
are considered \*(L"unsafe\*(R" for such purposes.
False by default.
.PP
\fIAllowCountByTokenAttributes\fR
.IX Subsection "AllowCountByTokenAttributes"
.PP
Unless this option is set to a true value,
any attempt to use a token-level attribute as a count-key (\f(CW\*(C`#by[..., $INDEX ...]\*(C'\fR)
will cause a runtime exception to be thrown.
True by default, can be disabled with e.g. \f(CW\*(C`AllowCountByTokenAttributes no\*(C'\fR.
.PP
\fIOutputBibliographyOfHits\fR
.IX Subsection "OutputBibliographyOfHits"
.PP
(from DDCReadme):
If set, then \s-1DDC\s0 outputs bibliographical information for hits instead of filenames.
.PP
\fILemmaQueryUsesMorphPattern\fR
.IX Subsection "LemmaQueryUsesMorphPattern"
.PP
If true (default), then \s-1DDC\s0 will treat \f(CW%LEMMA\fR queries as \f(CW\*(C`$Lemma=[LEMMA]\*(C'\fR,
i.e. implicitly insert @\-delimters around a regex \f(CW\*(C`LEMMA\*(C'\fR.
Otherwise, \f(CW%LEMMA\fR queries will be treated as \f(CW\*(C`$Lema=@LEMMA\*(C'\fR, i.e. literal matches.
Since ddc v2.0.20.
.PP
\fIIndexChunks\fR
.IX Subsection "IndexChunks"
.PP
(from DDCReadme):
If set, enables indexing and querying using 'chunks', otherwise chunks are ignored.
Only meaningful for the \f(CW\*(C`Free_Index\*(C'\fR Index Type.
.PP
\&\s-1NOTE:\s0 seems to control implicit creation of a \f(CW\*(C`Chunk\*(C'\fR index.
.PP
\fIIndexMorphPatterns\fR
.IX Subsection "IndexMorphPatterns"
.PP
(from DDCReadme):
If set, \s-1DDC\s0 creates a \f(CW\*(C`MorphPattern\*(C'\fR index.
Only meaningful for the \f(CW\*(C`DWDS_Index\*(C'\fR Index Type.
.PP
\fIIndexPunctuation\fR
.IX Subsection "IndexPunctuation"
.PP
(from DDCReadme):
Index punctuation marks only if set.
Only meaningful for the \f(CW\*(C`DWDS_Index\*(C'\fR Index Type.
.PP
\fIUseDwdsThesaurus\fR
.IX Subsection "UseDwdsThesaurus"
.PP
(from DDCReadme):
Enable creation and use of the \f(CW\*(C`Thes\*(C'\fR thesaurus index if set.
Only meaningful for the \f(CW\*(C`DWDS_Index\*(C'\fR Index Type.
.PP
\fIUseParagraphTagToDivide\fR
.IX Subsection "UseParagraphTagToDivide"
.PP
(from DDCReadme):
If set, the tokenizer seeks \f(CW\*(C`</p>\*(C'\fR in the input 
texts in order to  divide the text into paragraphs.
Only meaningful for the \f(CW\*(C`DWDS_Index\*(C'\fR Index Type.
.PP
\fIEmptyLineIsNotSentenceDelim\fR
.IX Subsection "EmptyLineIsNotSentenceDelim"
.PP
(from DDCReadme):
If set, an empty line in the input texts is not interpreted as an end of sentence.
Only meaningful for the \f(CW\*(C`DWDS_Index\*(C'\fR Index Type.
.PP
\fIDontUseIndention\fR
.IX Subsection "DontUseIndention"
.PP
(from DDCReadme):
If set, \s-1DDC \s0 doesn’t use indention (sic; assumedly \*(L"indentation\*(R" is meant) to find paragraph breaks.
Only meaningful for the \f(CW\*(C`DWDS_Index\*(C'\fR Index Type.
.PP
\fIArchiveIndex\fR
.IX Subsection "ArchiveIndex"
.PP
Undocumented
.PP
\fIResumeOnIndexErrors\fR
.IX Subsection "ResumeOnIndexErrors"
.PP
Undocumented
.PP
\fIGutenbergInterface\fR
.IX Subsection "GutenbergInterface"
.PP
Undocumented
.PP
\fIDwdsCorpusInterface\fR
.IX Subsection "DwdsCorpusInterface"
.PP
(from DDCReadme):
If set, enables DWDS-like formatting for output hits.
.SS "Single-valued Options"
.IX Subsection "Single-valued Options"
The following options take a single argument, which may be a list of values.
Each of these options should occur at most once in an opt-file.
Assumedly, in the case of multiple occurrences of the same option, the most recent
declaration \*(L"wins\*(R".
.PP
\fIIndices\fR
.IX Subsection "Indices"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& Indices INDEXLIST
.Ve
.PP
(largely drawn from DDCReadme):
Declares index fields used by the underlying corpus index.
\&\f(CW\*(C`INDEXLIST\*(C'\fR is a list  of index declarations delimited by semicolons \f(CW\*(C`;\*(C'\fR.
Each index declaration is a string of the form
.PP
.Vb 1
\& [LONGNAME SHORTNAME ARCHIVE STORAGE]
.Ve
.PP
where:
.IP "\s-1LONGNAME\s0" 4
.IX Item "LONGNAME"
is a long name for the index, conventionally in CamelCase;
.IP "\s-1SHORTNAME\s0" 4
.IX Item "SHORTNAME"
is a short name for the index, conventionally in all lower-case and frequently only a single character;
.IP "\s-1ARCHIVE\s0" 4
.IX Item "ARCHIVE"
is one of the strings \f(CW\*(C`archive\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`normal\*(C'\fR.
If it is \f(CW\*(C`archive\*(C'\fR, the current index is archived, otherwise it is not; and
.IP "\s-1STORAGE\s0" 4
.IX Item "STORAGE"
is one of the strings \f(CW\*(C`storage\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`storage_omit\*(C'\fR.
If it is \f(CW\*(C`storage\*(C'\fR, then the index is supplied with a storage during indexing, otherwise storage is not built.
By default the first index is built with a storage (if it is not \*(L"manually prohibited\*(R" (whatever that means)),
other indices are built without storages.
If you want your \s-1DDC\s0 index to \fBdisplay\fR values of this attribute for tokens matching a user query,
you should set this to \f(CW\*(C`storage\*(C'\fR.  If you're only interested in \fBquerying\fR this attribute,
you can safely set it to \f(CW\*(C`storage_omit\*(C'\fR.
.PP
The Indices option is only supported for the \f(CW\*(C`Free_Index\*(C'\fR Index Type.
.PP
\fIIndexAlias\fR
.IX Subsection "IndexAlias"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& IndexAlias FROM TO
.Ve
.PP
Defines a new token-level alias \f(CW\*(C`FROM\*(C'\fR for the existing index \f(CW\*(C`TO\*(C'\fR.
\&\f(CW\*(C`TO\*(C'\fR may be
a long index name declared in Indices,
a short index name declared in Indices,
or a valid index alias previously declared by another \f(CW\*(C`IndexAlias\*(C'\fR directive.
Causes all runtime query operations on the pseudo-index \f(CW\*(C`FROM\*(C'\fR to be evaluated
with respect to the underlying index \f(CW\*(C`TO\*(C'\fR.
Useful for facilitating interoperability of heterogeneous corpora.
.PP
Since v2.1.5
.PP
\fIIndicesToShow\fR
.IX Subsection "IndicesToShow"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& IndicesToShow SHOWLIST
.Ve
.PP
Declares a list of those indices which should be returned in hit responses to user queries.
\&\f(CW\*(C`SHOWLIST\*(C'\fR is a list of index keys, separated by whitespace, commata (',') or semicolons (';').  Each index label
in \f(CW\*(C`SHOWLIST\*(C'\fR can be either:
.IP "\(bu" 4
a long index name declared by Indices (since v2.1.5),
.IP "\(bu" 4
a short index name declared by Indices (since v2.1.5),
.IP "\(bu" 4
an index alias label declared by IndexAlias (since v2.1.5),
or
.IP "\(bu" 4
the integer position \fIi\fR of the corresponding index declaration in Indices, counting from 1;
i.e. for each \fIi\fR, 1 <= \fIi\fR <= N_INDICES, where N_INDICES is the number of index declarations by the Indices option.
.PP
By default, IndicesToShow is \f(CW1\fR, i.e. words are represented only by the value of the first index
(normally the text of the token itself). If some index is mentioned in IndicesToShow, then it must have an index storage built during indexing.
Prior to v2.1.5, \fB\s-1ONLY\s0\fR whitespace-separated integer positions were allowed for \f(CW\*(C`SHOWLIST\*(C'\fR.
.PP
Only supported for the \f(CW\*(C`Free_Index\*(C'\fR IndexType.
.PP
Support for long and short index names, aliases, and additional separators since v2.1.5.
.PP
\fIDefaultBibl\fR
.IX Subsection "DefaultBibl"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& DefaultBibl NAME
.Ve
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`NAME\*(C'\fR is the name of a fallback bibliographic field to be queried if no literal match is found for a runtime
(user) filter.  \f(CW\*(C`NAME\*(C'\fR must be the name of a bibliographic metadata attribute as defined by the
Bibl option.
This option can be used in conjunction with a \f(CW\*(C`constant\*(C'\fR bibliographic metadata attribute
to provide a default value for unknown bibliographic metadata attributes, e.g. to facilitate interoperability
between multiple heterogeneous corpora without the need for re-indexing or explicit definition of
Bibl constant fields.
If omitted or set to the empty string (the default), query filters on an undefined bibliographic
attribute will raise a runtime error.
.PP
\&\fB\s-1CAVEAT\s0\fR: You should think \fBcarefully\fR before using this feature, since it will suppress error and/or warning
messages due to typographical errors for \*(L"real\*(R" attribute fields.
.PP
Since v2.0.27
.PP
\fIHitBorders\fR
.IX Subsection "HitBorders"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& HitBorders BREAKLIST
.Ve
.PP
(largely from DDCReadme):
\&\f(CW\*(C`BREAKLIST\*(C'\fR is a string of break collection declarations delimited by semicolons \f(CW\*(C`;\*(C'\fR.
Each break collection declaration is a colon-separated string of the form
\&\f(CW\*(C`[LONGNAME:SHORTNAME]\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`[LONGNAME:SHORTNAME:default]\*(C'\fR, where
.IP "\s-1LONGNAME\s0" 4
.IX Item "LONGNAME"
is a long name for the break collection, conventionally in CamelCase;
.IP "\s-1SHORTNAME\s0" 4
.IX Item "SHORTNAME"
is a short name for the break collection, conventionally in all lower-case and frequently only a single character; and
.ie n .IP """default""" 4
.el .IP "\f(CWdefault\fR" 4
.IX Item "default"
is the literal string \f(CW\*(C`default\*(C'\fR, which if present indicates that the
current break collection is to be used for queries which do not specify any
break collection specification (e.g. using the \f(CW\*(C`#WITHIN\*(C'\fR query operator).
.PP
The HitBorders option is only supported for the \f(CW\*(C`Free_Index\*(C'\fR Index Type.
.PP
\fIHtmlHighlighting\fR
.IX Subsection "HtmlHighlighting"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& HtmlHighlighting TAGS
.Ve
.PP
Set highlighting strings to use for identifying matched tokens in hits returned in \s-1HTML\s0 format.
\&\f(CW\*(C`TAGS\*(C'\fR is a string of the form \f(CW\*(C`L;R;LL;RR\*(C'\fR, where
the first matched token \fIw\fR in any hit is marked by \f(CW\*(C`L\*(C'\fR\fIw\fR\f(CW\*(C`R\*(C'\fR,
and subsequent matched tokens in the same hit are marked as \f(CW\*(C`LL\*(C'\fR\fIw\fR\f(CW\*(C`RR\*(C'\fR.
Tag strings support C\-style escapes as well as JSON-style unicode (\s-1UTF\-8\s0) escapes.
.PP
Default is:
.PP
.Vb 1
\& HtmlHighlighting <STRONG><FONT COLOR=red>;</FONT></STRONG>;<STRONG><FONT COLOR=red>;</FONT></STRONG>
.Ve
.PP
\fITextHighlighting\fR
.IX Subsection "TextHighlighting"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& TextHighlighting TAGS
.Ve
.PP
Set highlighting strings to use for identifying matched tokens in hits returned in text format.
\&\f(CW\*(C`TAGS\*(C'\fR is string as described under HtmlHighlighting.
.PP
Default is:
.PP
.Vb 1
\& TextHighlighting &&;&&;_&;&_
.Ve
.PP
\fITableHighlighting\fR
.IX Subsection "TableHighlighting"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& TableHighlighting TAGS
.Ve
.PP
Set highlighting strings to use for identifying matched tokens in hits returned in table format.
\&\f(CW\*(C`TAGS\*(C'\fR is string as described under HtmlHighlighting.
.PP
Default is:
.PP
.Vb 1
\& TableHighlighting &&;&&;_&;&_
.Ve
.PP
\fITokenDelimiter\fR
.IX Subsection "TokenDelimiter"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& TokenDelimiter DELIM
.Ve
.PP
Set delimiter string to be inserted before the data for each token in \s-1HTML,\s0 Text, and Table output formats.
Prior to \s-1DDC\s0 release 2.0 (branch 1.80\-dx1), this option was not present and token boundaries could not be reliably
determined from the built-in output formats.
The string \s-1DELIM\s0 may not contain any literal whitespace, but C\-style escapes and
JSON-style unicode escapes are supported
.PP
For historical reasons, \s-1DELIM\s0 defaults to an empty string.
.PP
\fIInterpDelimiter\fR
.IX Subsection "InterpDelimiter"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& InterpDelimiter DELIM
.Ve
.PP
Set delimiter string to be inserted between individual index fields for each token in \s-1HTML,\s0 Text, and Table output formats.
The string \s-1DELIM\s0 may not contain any literal whitespace, but C\-style escapes and
JSON-style unicode escapes are supported
Defaults to \f(CW\*(C`#\*(C'\fR.
.PP
For historical reasons, the (misspelled) option \f(CW\*(C`InterpDelimeter\*(C'\fR is an alias for \f(CW\*(C`InterpDelimiter\*(C'\fR.
.PP
\fILeftKwicContextSize\fR
.IX Subsection "LeftKwicContextSize"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& LeftKwicContextSize N
.Ve
.PP
(from DDCReadme):
Set the length of the right context to use for each
\&\s-1KWIC\s0 line when generating file summaries (snippets).
The default value is 4.
.PP
\fIRightKwicContextSize\fR
.IX Subsection "RightKwicContextSize"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& RightKwicContextSize N
.Ve
.PP
(from DDCReadme):
Set the length of the right context to use for each
\&\s-1KWIC\s0 line when generating file summaries (snippets).
The default value is 4.
.PP
\fINumberOfKwicLinesInSnippets\fR
.IX Subsection "NumberOfKwicLinesInSnippets"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& NumberOfKwicLinesInSnippets N
.Ve
.PP
(from DDCReadme):
Set the number of kwic lines in snippets. The default value is 10.
.PP
\fIMaxRegExpExpansionSize\fR
.IX Subsection "MaxRegExpExpansionSize"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& MaxRegExpExpansionSize N
.Ve
.PP
(from DDCReadme):
Set the maximum number of indexed items
which can be included in an expansion set of one regular expression.
Default value is 1000000.
.PP
\fIMaxCachedHitsCount\fR
.IX Subsection "MaxCachedHitsCount"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& MaxCachedHitsCount N
.Ve
.PP
Set maximum number of hits to store in a cache entry of an associated CConcHolder (subcorpus server).
Query results with more than \f(CW\*(C`N\*(C'\fR hits will not be cached.
Default=512.
.PP
Since v2.0.23 (formerly a global constant = 500).
.PP
\fIMaxQueryCacheSize\fR
.IX Subsection "MaxQueryCacheSize"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& MaxQueryCacheSize N
.Ve
.PP
Set maximum number of queries to be LRU-cached by an associated CConcHolder (subcorpus server).
Default=512.
.PP
Since v2.0.23 (formerly a global constant = 500).
.PP
\fIUserMaxTokenCountInOnePeriod\fR
.IX Subsection "UserMaxTokenCountInOnePeriod"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& UserMaxTokenCountInOnePeriod N
.Ve
.PP
(from DDCReadme):
Set the size of internal subcorpus blocks.
The greater the value of this parameter is, the faster
querying procedures work, and the more memory the program needs.
.PP
This parameter is basically a block-size limit for so-called \*(L"periods\*(R"
(aka \*(L"partitions\*(R", \*(L"blocks\*(R") of a physical sucorpus
used implicitly by the low-level query
evaluation routines.
A \*(L"period\*(R" is a contiguous sequence of documents within a single
physical (sub)corpus.
\&\f(CW\*(C`CConcSession::GetAllHits()\*(C'\fR iterates over all \*(L"periods\*(R" of a physical
subcorpus, and (re\-)populates the set of query hits within the current \*(L"period\*(R"
at each step of the iteration.
Query filters (sort operators, \f(CW\*(C`#HAS_FIELD\*(C'\fR, etc.) are re-evaluated for each
period-local hit subset.  User-supplied timeout values and hint optimization
are only checked at the end of each \*(L"period\*(R"\-specific iteration.
.PP
This mechanism was probably originally meant to reduce the likelihood of \s-1RAM\s0 overflow for large
result-sets (e.g. function words) with nontrival filters (e.g. =#HAS[author,...]=) by restricting
the number of hits that had to be kept in memory at any given time, under the assumption
that the filter stage would substantially reduce the number of valid hits, but can lead
to longer query times especially for large corpora containing many \*(L"small\*(R" documents
in the presence of a non-trvial sort operator (e.g. \f(CW\*(C`#ASC_DATE\*(C'\fR).
.PP
Default if unspecified is hard-coded as 5000000 (5M).
.PP
\fIUserMaxInputLoadIndexSize\fR
.IX Subsection "UserMaxInputLoadIndexSize"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& UserMaxInputLoadIndexSize N
.Ve
.PP
Minimum number of tokens to buffer during corpus indexing before considering
flushing to disk and possibly introducing a period boundary.
Must be strictly less than \*(L"UserMaxTokenCountInOnePeriod\*(R", otherwise
defaults to \f(CW\*(C`"UserMaxTokenCountInOnePeriod"/10\*(C'\fR.
.PP
Global default if unspecified is hard-coded as 400000 (400K).
.PP
Since v2.2.0 (previously only a hard-coded constant).
.PP
\fILocalPathPrefix\fR
.IX Subsection "LocalPathPrefix"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& LocalPathPrefix STRING
.Ve
.PP
(from DDCReadme):
The common prefix of each corpus filename that should be replaced by
the value of InternetPathPrefix when \s-1DDC\s0 outputs file names.
.PP
\fIInternetPathPrefix\fR
.IX Subsection "InternetPathPrefix"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& InternetPathPrefix STRING
.Ve
.PP
Replaces the value of LocalPathPrefix in corpus filenames
if and when they occur in \s-1DDC\s0 output.
.PP
\fITfIdfRank\fR
.IX Subsection "TfIdfRank"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& TfIdfRank FLOAT
.Ve
.PP
(from DDCReadme):
Float parameter (0 <=FLOAT < 1) for \s-1TFIDF\s0 weighting.
.PP
\fIPositionRank\fR
.IX Subsection "PositionRank"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& PositionRank FLOAT
.Ve
.PP
(from DDCReadme):
Float parameter (0 <=FLOAT < 1) for position weighting.
.PP
\fINearRank\fR
.IX Subsection "NearRank"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& NearRank FLOAT
.Ve
.PP
Float parameter (0 <=FLOAT < 1) for \s-1NEAR\s0 weighting.
.PP
\fIServerInfo\fR
.IX Subsection "ServerInfo"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& ServerInfo KEY VALUE
.Ve
.PP
Sets a constant value to be returned in responses to client 'info' requests
to an associated CDDCLeafServer (see ddc_proto).  \s-1KEY\s0 is a key string optionally
containing C escapes, and \s-1VALUE\s0 is a literal \s-1JSON\s0 value to be returned as the value
of \f(CW\*(C`user.KEY\*(C'\fR in leaf-server 'info' responses.
.PP
Since v2.0.34.
.PP
\fIServerInfoFile\fR
.IX Subsection "ServerInfoFile"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& ServerInfoFile KEY FILENAME
.Ve
.PP
Sets an external filename value to be returned in responses to client 'info' requests
to an associated CDDCLeafServer (see ddc_proto).  \s-1KEY\s0 is a key string optionally
containing C escapes, and \s-1FILENAME\s0 is a filename containing literal \s-1JSON\s0 code to be returned as the value
of \f(CW\*(C`user.KEY\*(C'\fR in leaf-server 'info' responses.  \s-1FILENAME\s0 is interpreted relative to the directory
containing the project *.con file associated with the leaf server.
As of v2.1.5, leading and trailing whitespace will be implicitly trimmed from \s-1FILENAME.\s0
.PP
Since v2.0.34.
.SS "Multi-valued Options"
.IX Subsection "Multi-valued Options"
The following options may occur multiple times in a single opt-file.
.PP
\fItextarea\fR
.IX Subsection "textarea"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& textarea NAME XPATH
.Ve
.PP
(from DDCReadme):
Each \f(CW\*(C`textarea\*(C'\fR declaration describes a single text area, where
\&\s-1NAME\s0 is name of the text area field, and
\&\s-1XPATH\s0 is an X\-Path.
.PP
\fIBibl\fR
.IX Subsection "Bibl"
.PP
.Vb 2
\& Bibl alias            NAME VALUE
\& Bibl TYPE  VISIBILITY NAME VALUE
.Ve
.PP
(mostly from DDCReadme):
Each \f(CW\*(C`Bibl\*(C'\fR declaration describes a single bibliographic field (such as date of publication, author, and so on).
The bibliographic field can be predefined (\*(L"orig\*(R", \*(L"scan\*(R", \*(L"date\*(R", \*(L"page\*(R", \*(L"pagerank\*(R") or free (user-defined).
Predefined bibliographic fields have special processing in \s-1DDC,\s0 for example, field \*(L"scan\*(R" is used to build a hit
header. Free bibliographic fields can contain either integer or string data: for both datatypes, one can use
the general filter operator or general order operators (\f(CW\*(C`#HAS\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`#LESS_BY\*(C'\fR respectively).
The arguments to free \f(CW\*(C`Bibl\*(C'\fR are:
.IP "\s-1TYPE\s0" 4
.IX Item "TYPE"
is the type of the bibliographic field; either \f(CW\*(C`string\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`integer\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`constant\*(C'\fR, or \f(CW\*(C`alias\*(C'\fR.
.IP "\s-1VISIBILITY\s0" 4
.IX Item "VISIBILITY"
(only for non\-\f(CW\*(C`alias\*(C'\fR metadata fields);
either \f(CW1\fR or \f(CW0\fR: if it is \*(L"1\*(R",  then \s-1DDC\s0 displays the value of the field for each hit header.
For \f(CW\*(C`alias\*(C'\fR fields, the \f(CW\*(C`VISIBILITY\*(C'\fR flag should be omitted.
.IP "\s-1NAME\s0" 4
.IX Item "NAME"
a symbolic name for this bibliographic field (by convention all lower-case); and
.IP "\s-1VALUE\s0" 4
.IX Item "VALUE"
For the \f(CW\*(C`string\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`integer\*(C'\fR types, \f(CW\*(C`VALUE\*(C'\fR should be an X\-Path specification of the location
of the field data in corpus source documents; for
the \f(CW\*(C`constant\*(C'\fR type, it should be a literal string indicating the value to be returned,
and for the \f(CW\*(C`alias\*(C'\fR type, it should be the name of the target bibliographic field (or other alias)
for which the pseudo-attribute \f(CW\*(C`NAME\*(C'\fR is to serve as an alias.
.Sp
All X\-Path expressions for the document-dependent \f(CW\*(C`string\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`integer\*(C'\fR metadata types should be \*(L"trivial\*(R" in the
sense that for each input document, every metadata X\-Path \f(CW\*(C`VALUE\*(C'\fR should resolve to a unique attribute\- or element-node,
whose content should be a single text node containing all and only the string to be indexed as the value of the
metadata attribute \f(CW\*(C`NAME\*(C'\fR for that document (i.e. any nested elements and their content will be ignored).
For best results, the X\-Path should be an absolute
\&\f(CW\*(C`XPATH\*(C'\fR is an absolute location path (beginning with \f(CW\*(C`/\*(C'\fR), and consisting
only of element names (\f(CW\*(C`foo\*(C'\fR),
element wildcards (\f(CW\*(C`*\*(C'\fR),
slashes (\f(CW\*(C`/\*(C'\fR),
and attribute restriction clauses (\f(CW\*(C`[@foo="bar"]\*(C'\fR).
.Sp
A non-empty "\f(CW\*(C`date\*(C'\fR" attribute is \fBrequired\fR for all input documents,
and all metadata attribute values may be at most 20000 bytes (20 kB) in length:
longer values in input files will cause the \f(CW\*(C`ddc_index\*(C'\fR process to abort.
.PP
Note that additions, deletions, and/or changes to \f(CW\*(C`constant\*(C'\fR bibliographic fields do \fBnot\fR
require re-indexing, whereas any changes to non\-\f(CW\*(C`constant\*(C'\fR fields \fBdo\fR.
.PP
As of v2.1.5, leading and trailing whitespace will be implicitly trimmed from \f(CW\*(C`VALUE\*(C'\fR strings
for metadata fields of type "\f(CW\*(C`constant\*(C'\fR".
.PP
Prior to v2.0.30, multiple definitions of a bibliographic field \f(CW\*(C`NAME\*(C'\fR caused a fatal
error when loading a corpus project.  As of v2.0.30, a warning is emitted in these cases,
and the most recent definition is used (later definitions effectively \*(L"clobbering\*(R" earlier
ones).
.PP
Since v1.x; \f(CW\*(C`alias\*(C'\fR fields since v2.1.5, \f(CW\*(C`constant\*(C'\fR fields since v2.0.27, multiple definition since v2.0.30.
.PP
\fIBigrams\fR
.IX Subsection "Bigrams"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& Bigrams INDEXNAME MAXLEN BREAKNAME
.Ve
.PP
Request construction of an n\-gram index at indexing time, suitable for sorting runtime hits
lexicographically by neighbor \fI\s-1INDEXNAME\s0\fR using the \f(CW\*(C`#left\*(C'\fR and/or \f(CW\*(C`#right\*(C'\fR query operators.
Up to \fI\s-1MAXLEN\s0\fR neighbors are considered for the sort.  Breaks of type \fI\s-1BREAKNAME\s0\fR terminate
the sort.
Example usage: \f(CW\*(C`Bigrams Token 2 s\*(C'\fR.
.PP
\&\fB\s-1OBSOLETE\s0\fR as of ddc v2.0.19, which supports generic \f(CW\*(C`#left\*(C'\fR and/or \f(CW\*(C`#right\*(C'\fR query operators
on arbitrary token attributes within the current query break collection, provided the indices for 
the attributes in question were built with the \s-1STORAGE\s0 option set.
.PP
\fIExpand\fR
.IX Subsection "Expand"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& Expand LABEL CLASS PARAM...
.Ve
.PP
Declares a named term expander which can be used to expand index queries
using either the explicit pipeline notation (\f(CW\*(C`|LABEL|...\*(C'\fR), or implicit
expansion heuristics on a per-index basis.  Conceptually,
each expander in a pipeline operates on the set of terms returned by the
previous expander in the pipeline, and the set returned by the final expander
in the pipeline represents that set of index values which qualify as \*(L"matches\*(R" to the term queried.
.PP
\&\fI\s-1LABEL\s0\fR is a label string
used to identify the expander in user-specified pipelines.
If \fI\s-1LABEL\s0\fR is also the \fI\s-1LONGNAME\s0\fR of an index field declared in Indices,
then the corresponding expander is used implicitly if no explicit pipeline is
specified or if the default pipeline \f(CW\*(C`|\-\*(C'\fR is used.
.PP
\&\fI\s-1CLASS\s0\fR is a string representing the expansion function to use, and
\&\fI\s-1PARAM...\s0\fR are additional parameters to the expansion function.
Currently supported expander classes are:
.IP "Id" 4
.IX Item "Id"
.Vb 2
\& Expand LABEL Id
\& Expand LABEL Null
.Ve
.Sp
Identity expander (no-op).  Parameters: none.
.IP "Case" 4
.IX Item "Case"
.Vb 1
\&  Expand LABEL Case LANG
.Ve
.Sp
Letter-case expander (upper\- vs. lower-case).
Parameters: \f(CW\*(C`LANG\*(C'\fR, a language string as accepted by the
\&\s-1LANG\s0 declaration.  In particular, the
\&\*(L"language\*(R" \f(CW\*(C`Generic\*(C'\fR can be used to specify that
the C99 locale settings should be used to provide
upper/lower\-case mappings on wide character strings:
in this case, you must ensure that the \fI\s-1LC_CTYPE\s0\fR
enviornment variable for the \s-1DDC\s0 process is set
appropriately for the corpus and runtime query data.
.Sp
Note that not all letter-case variants are created
by this expander (\*(L"McTaggart problem\*(R").
.IP "ToLower" 4
.IX Item "ToLower"
.Vb 1
\&  Expand LABEL ToLower LANG
.Ve
.Sp
Forces all terms to lower-case.
Parameters: \f(CW\*(C`LANG\*(C'\fR, a language string as accepted by the
\&\s-1LANG\s0 declaration, or \f(CW\*(C`Generic\*(C'\fR to use the C99 locale settings.
.IP "ToUpper" 4
.IX Item "ToUpper"
.Vb 1
\&  Expand LABEL ToUpper LANG
.Ve
.Sp
Forces all terms to upper-case.
Parameters: \f(CW\*(C`LANG\*(C'\fR, a language string as accepted by the
\&\s-1LANG\s0 declaration, or \f(CW\*(C`Generic\*(C'\fR to use the C99 locale settings.
.IP "Infl" 4
.IX Item "Infl"
.Vb 2
\& Expand LABEL Infl LANG
\& Expand LABEL Morph LANG
.Ve
.Sp
Inflectional variant expander using built-in morphology
tables.  Parameters: \f(CW\*(C`LANG\*(C'\fR, a language string as
accepted by the \s-1LANG\s0 declaration.
.IP "Cab" 4
.IX Item "Cab"
.Vb 1
\& Expand LABEL Cab URL TIMEOUT DEBUG MAPMODE
.Ve
.Sp
Orthographic variant expander which queries an external \s-1DTA::CAB HTTP\s0 server.
Parameters:
.RS 4
.IP "\s-1URL\s0" 4
.IX Item "URL"
\&\s-1URL\s0 of the \s-1DTA::CAB HTTP\s0 server to be queried with \f(CW\*(C`GET\*(C'\fR request.
The underlying implementation appends a URL-encoded parameter \f(CW\*(C`qd=DATA\*(C'\fR
to this \s-1URL\s0 before requesting data from the server, where \fI\s-1DATA\s0\fR is
a newline-separated list of types to be expanded.
The data format returned by the server is assumed to be a list
of expanded types separated by TABs, newlines, and/or carriage returns.
Empty-string types in the output are ignored.
.Sp
As of ddc v2.1.8, ddc supports \s-1HTTP\s0 over \s-1UNIX\s0 domain sockets on the
local machine by means of specially formatted URLs:
.Sp
.Vb 4
\& http:/path/to/socket//request/uri        # perl LWP::Protocol::http::SocketUnixAlt style
\& unix:/path/to/socket|http:///request/uri # apache mod_proxy style
\& http+unix:/path/to/socket//request/uri   # native http+unix scheme, //\-separated
\& http+unix:/path/to/socket|/request/uri   # native http+unix scheme, |\-separated
.Ve
.Sp
\&... all of these \s-1URL\s0 formats should cause ddc to query the \s-1HTTP\s0 server
listening on the unix socket \fI/path/to/socket\fR with a \f(CW\*(C`GET\*(C'\fR request
for the \s-1URI \s0\fI/request/uri\fR.
.IP "\s-1TIMEOUT\s0" 4
.IX Item "TIMEOUT"
Timeout in seconds for the expansion query.
If the timeout is exceeded, a warning is printed and the
\&\s-1CAB\s0 expander behaves like an Id expander, returning
the same set it was passed.
.IP "\s-1DEBUG\s0" 4
.IX Item "DEBUG"
Boolean flag for debugging.  If set to a true value, all data
passed to and from the external \s-1DTA::CAB\s0 server will be echoed
to stderr.  Not for production use.
.IP "\s-1MAPMODE\s0" 4
.IX Item "MAPMODE"
Boolean flag.  If false or unspecified (\*(L"union-mode\*(R"), all input terms are
implicitly included to the output set, regardless of whether or not they are
also present in the server's response.  If \fI\s-1MAPMODE\s0\fR specified and true, only those
terms explicitly included in the server's response are included in the output set.
.RE
.RS 4
.RE
.IP "CabMap" 4
.IX Item "CabMap"
.Vb 1
\& Expand LABEL CabMap URL TIMEOUT DEBUG MAPMODE
.Ve
.Sp
Wrapper for the Cab class with a default \fI\s-1MAPMODE\s0\fR=1.
.IP "Chain" 4
.IX Item "Chain"
.Vb 1
\& Expand LABEL Chain PIPELINE...
.Ve
.Sp
Assigns a label to a chain of previously defined expanders.
Takes a \f(CW\*(C`PIPELINE\*(C'\fR of expander labels as its argument:
expander labels in \f(CW\*(C`PIPELINE\*(C'\fR may separated by whitespace
and/or the \f(CW\*(C`|\*(C'\fR symbol (multiple consecutive delimiters are ignored).
Note that an empty expander chain is equivalent to an Id expander.
.PP
\&\s-1DDC\s0 ensures that the following expander labels are defined,
instantiating them with default parameters only if no other
expander with the same label is explicitly defined in the opt-file:
.IP "id" 4
.IX Item "id"
Defined as \f(CW\*(C`Expand id Id\*(C'\fR
.IP "null" 4
.IX Item "null"
Defined as \f(CW\*(C`Expand null Id\*(C'\fR
.IP "case" 4
.IX Item "case"
Defined as \f(CW\*(C`Expand case LANG\*(C'\fR unless the Utf8 flag was set,
in which case the default \fIcase\fR expander is defined as \f(CW\*(C`Expand case Generic\*(C'\fR.
.IP "infl" 4
.IX Item "infl"
Defined as \f(CW\*(C`Expand infl LANG\*(C'\fR.
.IP "Token" 4
.IX Item "Token"
Default expansion chain for the \fIToken\fR index.
Usually defined as \f(CW\*(C`Expand Token Chain infl case\*(C'\fR,
but note that the \f(CW\*(C`case\*(C'\fR component will be omitted from
the default chain unless the legacy CaseInsensitive flag is set,
and the \f(CW\*(C`infl\*(C'\fR component will be omitted if
the legacy DisableDefaultQueryLexicalExpansion
option is set.
.PP
\fIExpandBibl\fR
.IX Subsection "ExpandBibl"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& ExpandBibl LABEL TARGET CLASS PARAM...
.Ve
.PP
Declares a named bibliographic expander which can be used in place of a
physically indexed bibliographic fields (as declared with the Bibl option)
in #HAS_FIELD queries.
.PP
\&\fI\s-1LABEL\s0\fR is a label string
used to identify the expander in user-specified pipelines.
\&\fI\s-1TARGET\s0\fR is the unique \fI\s-1NAME\s0\fR associated with a physically indexed bibliographic field used for the underlying query.
If \fI\s-1LABEL\s0\fR is also the \fI\s-1NAME\s0\fR of a physically indexed bibliographic field declared with Bibl,
then the pseudo-field declared with \f(CW\*(C`ExpandBibl\*(C'\fR has precedence when evaluating user queries.
.PP
\&\fI\s-1CLASS\s0\fR is a string representing the expansion function to use, and
\&\fI\s-1PARAM...\s0\fR are additional parameters to the expansion function.
Currently (ddc v2.0.5), all \fI\s-1CLASS\s0\fRes supported by the Expand option are also supported
by \f(CW\*(C`ExpandBibl\*(C'\fR, except for the \f(CW\*(C`Chain|/chain\*(C'\fR class.
.PP
No bibliographic expanders are defined by default.
.PP
\fIDefaultQueryIndex\fR
.IX Subsection "DefaultQueryIndex"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& DefaultQueryIndex OPKEY INDEXNAME
.Ve
.PP
Use index \f(CW\*(C`INDEXNAME\*(C'\fR if otherwise unspecified for runtime queries using operator \f(CW\*(C`OPKEY\*(C'\fR.
Known values for \f(CW\*(C`OPKEY\*(C'\fR, the associated query classes, and the associated default values for \f(CW\*(C`INDEXNAME\*(C'\fR
are:
.PP
.Vb 12
\&  OPKEY (INDEXNAME)     CLASS...
\& \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
\&   _    (Token)         CQTokInfl
\&  @_    (Token)         CQTokExact
\&  %_    (Lemma)         CQTokLemma
\&  /_/   (Token)         CQTokRegex, CQTokSuffix, CQTokPrefix, CQTokInfix
\&  [_]   (MorphPattern)  CQTokMorph
\& :{_}   (Thes)          CQTokThes
\&  ^_    (Chunk)         CQTokChunk
\&  <_    (Token)         CQTokFile
\&   *    (Token)         none (generic fallback)
\&   .    n/a             CQTokAnchor
.Ve
.PP
The \*(L".\*(R" operator key is used by $. queries; its \f(CW\*(C`INDEXNAME\*(C'\fR should resolve to a break name rather than a
token-level index name. Default is whatever break collection was declared as the default.
